  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mahesha.R vs Gowrish.N on 5 August, 2025 Kabc020053722022 In The Court Of The Iv Addl. Judge And Member, Motor Vehicle Accident Tribunal & Acjm, Bengaluru (Scch-6) Present: Smt. Chetana S.F. B.A., L.L.B., Iv Addl., Judge & Mact & Acjm, Court of Small Causes, Bengaluru. Dated This The 5th Day Of August 2025 M.V.C.No.953 Of 2022 Mahesha R S/o Rajappa K.T. Aged about 32 years Residing at Patelardoddi village, Achalu, Sathanuru hobli, Kanakapura Taluk Ramanagara district. (By Sri.G.C.RAJESH- Advocate) .. Petitioner - Versus - 1. Gowrish N S/o Narayana swamy, major R/at Harappanahalli village Gigani, Anekal Taluk Bengaluru rural district Scch-6 2 Mvc.953/2022 (Rc owner of Lgv Reg.No.KA-51-7566) 2. Icici Lombard General Insurance co., Ltd., Official No.121, The estate building, 9th floor, Dikenson road, Mg road, Bangalore-560042 Policy No.SF849322 from 24-8-2021 to 23-8-2022 3. Venkatesh M S/o Murthy M Aged about 28 years Mahesha.R vs Gowrish.N on 5 August, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111692626/ 1 R/at near Channakeshwara temple, Haragadde village, Jigani, Anekal Taluk Bengaluru rural district (Driver of offending vehicle) (By Sri.UMESHA R. - Advocate) ..... Respondents Judgment",
    "facts": "This is a petition filed u/s.166 of Indian Motor Vehicles Act, claiming for compensation of Rs.25,00,000/- with interest from respondents on account of injuries sustained by the petitioner in the road traffic accident. On 11-1-2022 at about 4.20 p.m. when the petitioner was riding his motor cycle bearing reg.No.KA-51-HG-1422 from Koppa side towards Nisarga layout side, Harappanahalli circle, Jigani, Anekal, Bangalore, at that time, driver of the light goods vehicle bearing No.KA-51- 7566 came from Jigani side towards Bannerghatta side with high speed and rash and negligent manner and dashed against the petitioner's motor cycle, as a result of which petitioner sustained severe injuries and took treatment at hospital as inpatient and spent money for his treatment. Prior to the accident, petitioner was hale and healthy and doing fruits and vegetable business and earning Rs.30,000/-. Due to accidental injuries, he lost the earnings and suffering from disabilities and discomforts. Hence prays to award compensation of Rs.20 lakhs from the respondents. 3. In response of Tribunal process, the respondent No.3 remained absent and placed exparte and respondent No.1 and 2 appeared through their respective counsel and filed their respective written statements. 4. The respondent No.1 in his written statement contended that the petitioner was not lodged the complaint immediately, because of petitioner himself was responsible for the accident, the petitioner was ridden the motor cycle with high speed and in a rash and negligent manner and hit against the Tata 709 vehicle. Hence prays to dismiss the petition. 5. The respondent No.2 contended that driver of the offending vehicle has no valid and effective DL at the time of accident or letting the vehicle to a person who does not have DL is clear violation of terms and conditions of the policy. The offending vehicle was used in a public place without having valid permit and fitness certificate there is a violation of Section 149(2), 134(c) and 158(6) of MV Act. The compensation claimed by the petitioner is excessive and contrary to section 3 of interest Act. The offending vehicle is not at all involved in the accident and it was intentionally implicated in Mahesha.R vs Gowrish.N on 5 August, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111692626/ 2 collusion with the driver, owner and other authorities to get the unlawful benefit from the insurance company by filing 2 days delayed complaint. The petitioner examined himself as P.W1 and examined two witnesses as PWs.2 and 3 and got marked Exs.P1 to P30 documents. The respondent No.1 got examined himself as RW-1 and got marked Ex-R1 and 2. 8. It was argued by the learned counsel for the respondent No.2 that there was a delay of two days in filing the complaint and the complainant has not stated the vehicle number which caused the accident in the complaint and also FIR. Complainant only stated that one Brown colour 709 goods vehicle has caused the accident and sped away from the spot. .N on 5 August, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111692626/ 3 has filed the false charge sheet against the offending vehicle and hence prays to dismiss the case.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the respondent argued that one unknown vehicle has caused the accident and it is hit and run case and in order to obtain the compensation, petitioner colluding with the respondent No.1 owner of the vehicle and IO Mahesha. R vs Gowrish 17. At this juncture, It is necessary to apply the citation reported in AIR 2011 SC 1504 between Parmeshwari -vs- Amir Chand & Ors - wherein it has been held that - \"In a situation of this nature, the Tribunal has rightly taken a holistic view of the matter. 19. In addenda, in a claim for compensation U/Sec.166 of M.V Act, 1988, the claimant has to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by the Hon'ble Apex Court in 'MANGALA RAM VS. ORIENTAL INSURANCE COMPANY reported in (2018) 5 SCC 656. it Be that as it may, the law is well settled that it is the impact of the physical disability on the particular avocation of the petitioner which is relevant for the purpose of assessment of compensation under the head of loss of future income as held by the Hon'ble Apex Court in Rajkumar's case reported in (2011) 1 SCC 343.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "17. At this juncture, It is necessary to apply the citation reported in AIR 2011 SC 1504 between Parmeshwari -vs- Amir Chand & Ors - wherein it has been held that - \"In a situation of this nature, the Tribunal has rightly taken a holistic view of the matter.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Learned counsel for the respondent argued that one unknown vehicle has caused the accident and it is hit and run case and in order to obtain the compensation, petitioner colluding with the respondent No.1 owner of the vehicle and IO Mahesha. R vs Gowrish",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "19. In addenda, in a claim for compensation U/Sec.166 of M.V Act, 1988, the claimant has to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by the Hon'ble Apex Court in 'MANGALA RAM VS. ORIENTAL INSURANCE COMPANY reported in (2018) 5 SCC 656. it Be that as it may, the law is well settled that it is the impact of the physical disability on the particular avocation of the petitioner which is relevant for the purpose of assessment of compensation under the head of loss of future income as held by the Hon'ble Apex Court in Rajkumar's case reported in (2011) 1 SCC 343."
  },
